247
00:16:05,641 --> 00:16:08,940
the combines mechanized the harvest.

248
00:16:09,040 --> 00:16:13,880
It replaced the hand operations of cutting the grain,

249
00:16:13,980 --> 00:16:16,280
stacking it in the field, letting it dry,

250
00:16:16,380 --> 00:16:19,580
and then hauling the bundles of grain to a thrasher

251
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:21,880
where they'd separate the straw from the grain.

252
00:16:21,980 --> 00:16:26,200
So the combine combined all those operations at once.

253
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:31,800
And that reduced labor use quite considerably in rice harvest.

254
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:34,140
Mechanically, the modern rice combine

255
00:16:34,141 --> 00:16:36,140
principles as the wheat combine.

256
00:16:36,141 --> 00:16:39,140
The difference is in the ground.

257
00:16:39,141 --> 00:16:43,140
Rice grows in fields submerged in water.

258
00:16:43,141 --> 00:16:47,140
The water is later drained, so the combines can begin the harvest.

259
00:16:47,141 --> 00:16:50,140
Because the ground is saturated, the first rice combines use

260
00:16:50,141 --> 00:16:54,140
tracks to cope with the muddy terrain.

261
00:16:54,141 --> 00:16:57,140
Today's combine can overcome the mud with wide, high-traction

262
00:16:57,141 --> 00:16:59,140
tires.

263
00:16:59,141 --> 00:17:03,140
In a 10-hour day, this machine can produce up to six tons of

264
00:17:03,141 --> 00:17:07,480
tons of clean rice. Modern combines have pushed high

265
00:17:07,481 --> 00:17:11,740
capacity harvesting beyond what was ever dreamed possible.

266
00:17:11,741 --> 00:17:15,380
The new challenge is equipping the massive machines with tools

267
00:17:15,381 --> 00:17:18,780
that gather intelligence. Computer controlled monitors

268
00:17:18,781 --> 00:17:22,700
and sensors are driving a new farming philosophy called

269
00:17:22,701 --> 00:17:25,800
precision agriculture. The concept of precision

270
00:17:25,801 --> 00:17:30,540
agriculture is one that is trying to allow a farmer to farm on a

271
00:17:30,541 --> 00:17:33,580
scale the way you garden in your backyard on a more intimate

272
00:17:33,581 --> 00:17:36,540
scale. Today's combine has the

273
00:17:36,541 --> 00:17:39,580
ability to measure the quantity and the moisture content of the

274
00:17:39,581 --> 00:17:43,660
crop on the fly. Information the farmer can use to make

275
00:17:43,661 --> 00:17:47,340
adjustments and ensure the crop he is harvesting is at peak

276
00:17:47,341 --> 00:17:51,180
condition. The machine can also use global

277
00:17:51,181 --> 00:17:55,260
positioning devices to map the entire field and track the

278
00:17:55,261 --> 00:17:59,500
combine's location in it. By producing a yield map, the

279
00:17:59,501 --> 00:18:02,200
the farmer can determine which areas of the field are producing

280
00:18:02,201 --> 00:18:05,240
less and for what reason.

281
00:18:05,241 --> 00:18:08,100
Using this precision intelligence,

282
00:18:08,101 --> 00:18:10,300
he can plot ways to improve weak spots

283
00:18:10,301 --> 00:18:15,040
for a more successful harvest in coming years.

284
00:18:15,041 --> 00:18:17,440
The harvesting of field crops may have become

285
00:18:17,441 --> 00:18:20,120
a satellite-assisted operation.

286
00:18:20,121 --> 00:18:23,240
But while impressive, such high-tech progression

287
00:18:23,241 --> 00:18:25,840
is irrelevant to hundreds of other crops,

288
00:18:25,841 --> 00:18:28,280
including the sugar beet.

289
00:18:28,281 --> 00:18:31,760
sugar beets are root crops that grow and must be extracted from

290
00:18:31,761 --> 00:18:35,260
beneath the ground. The sugar beet is a much desired

291
00:18:35,261 --> 00:18:40,460
crop in a sugar-obsessed world. Extracting pure sucrose from a

292
00:18:40,461 --> 00:18:44,000
sugar beet is a massive enterprise of traffic control and

293
00:18:44,001 --> 00:18:47,000
basic chemistry. Centralized sugar processing

294
00:18:47,001 --> 00:18:50,760
plants coordinate a beet harvest season that can last more than

295
00:18:50,761 --> 00:18:53,900
six months. Basically, sugar beets are

296
00:18:53,901 --> 00:18:57,900
in what's called the campaign. The factory needs about 4,000

297
00:18:57,901 --> 00:19:01,900
tons a day to operate, and those 4,000 tons are assembled from

298
00:19:01,901 --> 00:19:05,900
various growers' fields, usually in a particular area at a given

299
00:19:05,901 --> 00:19:08,900
time of year. Sugar beet harvesting was a

300
00:19:08,901 --> 00:19:12,900
one-at-a-time job that required a deft touch and a strong back.

301
00:19:12,901 --> 00:19:16,900
Sugar beets grow just beneath the surface, and workers used

302
00:19:16,901 --> 00:19:21,900
pitchforks to flip or pop the beets from the ground.

303
00:19:21,901 --> 00:19:25,040
harvesting begins after the leafy tops of the beet plants

304
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:32,140
are cut or flailed. Once they're flailed,

305
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:35,140
then the harvester is brought into the field, four rows

306
00:19:35,220 --> 00:19:38,140
typically, and they have lifter wheels.

307
00:19:38,220 --> 00:19:42,060
Two 29-inch in diameter pre-hardened steel lifter

308
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:47,420
wheels rotate at a 65-degree angle and pressure or pinch the

309
00:19:47,421 --> 00:19:51,160
or pinch the sugar beets out of the ground.

310
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:53,560
They come right down below the beets and lift it right up

311
00:19:53,660 --> 00:19:56,860
and drop it onto a conveyor belt, which rolls around the big drum,

312
00:19:56,960 --> 00:19:59,760
which helps knock off any loose soil, because you don't want to

313
00:19:59,860 --> 00:20:02,760
ship soil to the factory. A ferris wheel

314
00:20:02,860 --> 00:20:05,980
conveyor then delivers the beets to a parade of trucks,

315
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:09,080
waiting to speed the crop to the processing plant.

316
00:20:09,180 --> 00:20:12,680
100 pounds of beets will give you 15 pounds of sugar.

317
00:20:12,780 --> 00:20:16,420
Every 100 pounds, by the time you get the 48,000 pounds

318
00:20:16,421 --> 00:20:20,220
which is about a load of beets, you got over 7,000 pounds of

319
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:22,520
sugar in that truckload.

320
00:20:22,620 --> 00:20:26,160
In addition to processed sugar,

321
00:20:26,260 --> 00:20:30,460
sugar beet juice produces molasses, while the pulp provides

322
00:20:30,560 --> 00:20:33,780
a high-quality feed for dairy animals.

323
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:36,080
So it's a very complete utilization.

324
00:20:36,180 --> 00:20:38,780
I like to call the sugar beet the hog of the plant world.

325
00:20:38,880 --> 00:20:41,380
Every part is used.

326
00:20:41,480 --> 00:20:44,220
Mechanization has transformed the world of field

327
00:20:44,221 --> 00:20:46,620
field and root crop harvesting.

328
00:20:46,720 --> 00:20:49,160
But when a crop is soft and delicate,

329
00:20:49,260 --> 00:20:51,220
machines need a little help.

330
00:20:51,320 --> 00:20:54,960
In 1965, a tomato with a thick skin

331
00:20:55,060 --> 00:20:57,160
came to the rescue of a failing industry.

332
00:21:00,980 --> 00:21:03,180
More than one half the sugar produced in the US

333
00:21:03,280 --> 00:21:05,180
comes from sugar beets.

334
00:21:05,280 --> 00:21:08,220
The rest comes from sugar cane.

335
00:21:08,320 --> 00:21:12,220
Harvesting will return on Modern Marvels.

336
00:21:14,220 --> 00:21:19,220
At thousands of farmers markets and supermarkets,

337
00:21:19,221 --> 00:21:23,220
consumers expect to find fresh, hand-picked fruits and vegetables.

338
00:21:23,221 --> 00:21:26,220
And in most every case, that's exactly what they get.

339
00:21:26,221 --> 00:21:30,220
Fresh market produce is a hand-harvested commodity.

340
00:21:30,221 --> 00:21:34,220
Mechanized harvesting is typically reserved for those fruits and vegetables

341
00:21:34,221 --> 00:21:39,220
destined for processed products, such as juices, sauces, soups,

342
00:21:39,221 --> 00:21:43,220
and a host of preserved canned goods.

343
00:21:43,221 --> 00:21:49,060
The economic impact of mechanized harvesting is nowhere more obvious than the processed tomato.

344
00:21:50,340 --> 00:21:56,260
Rising costs and a labor shortage in the 1960s prompted scientists at the University of California

345
00:21:56,261 --> 00:21:59,710
at Davis to seek a mechanical solution.

346
00:21:59,711 --> 00:22:03,780
But tomatoes had special needs, so researchers

347
00:22:03,781 --> 00:22:07,260
turned to age-old plant breeding techniques.

348
00:22:07,261 --> 00:22:10,660
The problem was to develop a harvester that

349
00:22:10,661 --> 00:22:12,420
that didn't destroy the tomatoes.

350
00:22:12,421 --> 00:22:13,900
Tomatoes aren't like wheat.

351
00:22:13,901 --> 00:22:17,160
Tomatoes are soft and fuzzy creatures,

352
00:22:17,161 --> 00:22:20,720
and you can't go out there and mash them in the field.

353
00:22:20,721 --> 00:22:23,600
It required a tomato that had relatively tough skin

354
00:22:23,601 --> 00:22:27,000
and was resistant to being handled by the harvester.

355
00:22:28,020 --> 00:22:30,960
With tomatoes, they had to develop a variety

356
00:22:30,961 --> 00:22:34,160
that matured all at the same time,

357
00:22:34,161 --> 00:22:36,260
rather than stretched out over time,

358
00:22:36,261 --> 00:22:39,260
because they developed a system of mass harvesting

359
00:22:39,261 --> 00:22:40,500
everything at once.

360
00:22:40,600 --> 00:22:43,740
So they had to have all the tomatoes red at the same time.

361
00:22:43,840 --> 00:22:47,440
The thick-skinned fruit saved an industry.

362
00:22:47,540 --> 00:22:49,240
Today, the machine-friendly tomato

363
00:22:49,340 --> 00:22:52,240
is planted extensively, and production costs have decreased

364
00:22:52,340 --> 00:22:56,180
by over 30%.

365
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:58,620
The dusty task of picking and sorting

366
00:22:58,720 --> 00:23:01,780
used to require an army of laborers.

367
00:23:01,880 --> 00:23:04,080
The modern tomato harvester and four workers

368
00:23:04,180 --> 00:23:06,960
can pluck nearly 50 tons per hour.

369
00:23:06,961 --> 00:23:09,600
the machine lifts and consumes the entire vine

370
00:23:09,700 --> 00:23:12,300
as it moves through the field.

371
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:14,900
As the tomato vine enters the harvester,

372
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:18,800
it passes through a four-inch gap that strips off the dirt.

373
00:23:18,900 --> 00:23:22,640
A large brush then separates tomato from vine.

374
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:26,220
The vines are mulched and spit back into the field.

375
00:23:26,320 --> 00:23:30,420
Next, color sensors use photoelectric cells

376
00:23:30,520 --> 00:23:35,460
to distinguish between ripe red and unripe green tomatoes.

377
00:23:35,461 --> 00:23:38,960
The green tomatoes are ejected back into the field as fertilizer.

378
00:23:44,460 --> 00:23:47,000
Human sorters perform a final quality check

379
00:23:47,100 --> 00:23:49,000
before the tomatoes exit the harvester

380
00:23:49,100 --> 00:23:51,500
into a constant stream of waiting truck trailers.

381
00:23:54,520 --> 00:23:57,820
In a 90-day harvest period, a typical harvester

382
00:23:57,920 --> 00:24:00,160
operates around the clock, ultimately

383
00:24:00,260 --> 00:24:04,520
picking approximately 70,000 tons of soon-to-be processed

384
00:24:04,521 --> 00:24:09,820
tomatoes. Innovations like mechanical

385
00:24:09,821 --> 00:24:12,220
harvest have allowed that industry to stay in business,

386
00:24:12,221 --> 00:24:17,360
and that certainly is one of the key motivators for growers to

387
00:24:17,361 --> 00:24:22,280
look at mechanical harvest. For some crops, the

388
00:24:22,281 --> 00:24:25,980
transition to a mechanical harvest was a simple one.

389
00:24:25,981 --> 00:24:29,520
Nowhere is this more the case than with a nut harvest.

390
00:24:29,521 --> 00:24:34,520
Nuts have hard shells, and careful handling isn't an issue.

391
00:24:34,521 --> 00:24:40,900
In North America, Old World walnut trees were first planted in the West when Spanish Franciscan

392
00:24:40,901 --> 00:24:45,696
priests established the California Missions in the late 1700s.

393
00:24:45,697 --> 00:24:47,940
These first trees sprouted an

394
00:24:47,941 --> 00:24:53,343
industry that thrives today. Walnut harvesting was traditionally a hand operation.

395
00:24:53,344 --> 00:24:54,200
Workers using

396
00:24:54,201 --> 00:25:00,500
hooked poles shook individual branches, while oftentimes women and children stuffed the sacks.

397
00:25:00,501 --> 00:25:05,100
In the 1940s, a unique style of harvesting replaced the hooked

398
00:25:05,101 --> 00:25:10,340
pole with a variety of machine-driven limb shakers.

399
00:25:10,341 --> 00:25:13,540
Today, the mechanized walnut shaker moves quickly throughout

400
00:25:13,541 --> 00:25:15,540
the orchard on a single mission.

401
00:25:20,420 --> 00:25:24,460
It's designed to grasp the trunk of the tree and shake the whole

402
00:25:24,461 --> 00:25:28,060
tree at one time. Today's trees are also designed

403
00:25:28,061 --> 00:25:31,360
designed to accommodate the new shakers, unlike the older,

404
00:25:31,460 --> 00:25:34,800
larger trees that we have to shake individual limbs.

405
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:36,500
The shaking is actually performed

406
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:39,400
by counter-rotating weights in the shaker's clamp arm.

407
00:25:42,700 --> 00:25:45,120
When the vibrating begins, the spinning weights

408
00:25:45,220 --> 00:25:47,520
create a shaking pattern that transfers energy

409
00:25:47,620 --> 00:25:48,620
into the trunk.

410
00:25:58,060 --> 00:26:02,060
The shaker is closely followed by a swarm of support vehicles

411
00:26:02,061 --> 00:26:08,060
intent on finishing the job. Once they're on the ground,

412
00:26:08,061 --> 00:26:12,060
we have mechanical sweepers that come in and sweep the product

413
00:26:12,061 --> 00:26:16,060
into a windrow or a narrower row of walnuts.

414
00:26:16,061 --> 00:26:19,060
Once they're into a narrower windrow, then we have a

415
00:26:19,061 --> 00:26:23,060
harvesting machine that comes in and physically picks them up

416
00:26:23,061 --> 00:26:26,060
off the ground and puts them into a cart behind the

417
00:26:26,061 --> 00:26:28,860
behind the harvester.

418
00:26:28,861 --> 00:26:31,100
A shuttle system ferries loads of walnuts

419
00:26:31,101 --> 00:26:34,280
between the harvesters and truck trailers,

420
00:26:34,281 --> 00:26:35,700
where the nuts begin their journey

421
00:26:35,701 --> 00:26:38,740
to the dehydrating plant.

422
00:26:38,741 --> 00:26:40,440
On average, an acre of healthy trees

423
00:26:40,441 --> 00:26:44,820
will produce 2 and 1⁄2 tons of walnuts.

424
00:26:44,821 --> 00:26:48,860
Mechanically harvested nuts benefit from a hard outer hull.

425
00:26:48,861 --> 00:26:51,060
The fall does little to damage the final product.

426
00:26:56,060 --> 00:27:01,060
In the case of the black ripe olive, hand picking is still

427
00:27:01,061 --> 00:27:05,060
the prevailing method, because it decreases the chance of

428
00:27:05,061 --> 00:27:08,060
bruising the delicate fruit. But with harvest costs as high

429
00:27:08,061 --> 00:27:13,060
as 60% of total production, some growers are turning to

430
00:27:13,061 --> 00:27:19,060
sophisticated shaker harvesters. The canopy shaker features

431
00:27:19,061 --> 00:27:23,060
fiberglass picking rods that shake the tree branches, causing

432
00:27:23,061 --> 00:27:30,060
to detach and fall. An operator guides the picking

433
00:27:30,061 --> 00:27:35,060
rods into position. The rods oscillate at 250 revolutions

434
00:27:35,061 --> 00:27:39,060
per minute as the harvester creeps forward at 3 miles per

435
00:27:39,061 --> 00:27:43,060
hour. The olives fall onto padded belts

436
00:27:43,061 --> 00:27:47,060
and are then conveyed through an air blast separator, which blows

437
00:27:47,061 --> 00:27:53,060
out the leaves and twigs. The olives are deposited in the

438
00:27:53,061 --> 00:27:56,100
bins. On average, an acre of trees will

439
00:27:56,101 --> 00:28:00,460
produce four tons of olives. But the harvest is far from

440
00:28:00,461 --> 00:28:04,300
perfect. Presently, we're able to capture

441
00:28:04,301 --> 00:28:08,800
approximately 80% of the fruit on the tree.

442
00:28:08,801 --> 00:28:13,420
In comparison, a hand crew probably gets 90% to 95% of the

443
00:28:13,421 --> 00:28:17,760
fruit. So we lose a little production by leaving it on the

444
00:28:17,761 --> 00:28:22,260
tree, but we're able to do it at such an economic advantage

445
00:28:22,261 --> 00:28:27,260
that there's more dollars at the end of the year for us.

446
00:28:27,360 --> 00:28:29,660
Like olives, the hand harvesting

447
00:28:29,760 --> 00:28:32,260
of many fruit and vegetable crops is difficult

448
00:28:32,360 --> 00:28:35,100
and becoming more costly to growers.

449
00:28:35,200 --> 00:28:38,900
But in some cases, using technology to assist

450
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,020
rather than replace workers makes

451
00:28:41,120 --> 00:28:42,920
for a highly productive harvest.

452
00:28:46,960 --> 00:28:52,260
In 1919, botulism killed 35 people who ate impromptu.

453
00:28:52,261 --> 00:28:56,700
California black olives. It took 10 years and safer canning

454
00:28:56,701 --> 00:29:00,260
methods to reverse plummeting sales.

455
00:29:00,261 --> 00:29:04,260
Harvesting will return on Modern Marvels.

456
00:29:08,260 --> 00:29:11,260
Central California's San Joaquin Valley is the richest farm

457
00:29:11,261 --> 00:29:15,260
belt in the world. Its mild climate and nearly 6 million

458
00:29:15,261 --> 00:29:20,260
acres of farmland play host to some 350 different crops.

459
00:29:20,261 --> 00:29:25,060
Harvesting these crops requires a delicate balance of mechanization

460
00:29:25,061 --> 00:29:30,060
and hand labor, where machines can't feasibly do the work.

461
00:29:30,061 --> 00:29:33,620
Farm laborers, usually hired on a temporary basis, provide the

462
00:29:33,621 --> 00:29:37,620
muscle. For decades, California has depended

463
00:29:37,621 --> 00:29:41,620
on its neighbor to the south to supply the workers.

464
00:29:41,621 --> 00:29:45,620
The stream of Mexican labor began during World War II, when

465
00:29:45,621 --> 00:29:49,620
Franklin Delano Roosevelt initiated the Bracero Program.

466
00:29:49,621 --> 00:29:53,620
Known as the strong-armed ones, the Braceros were a temporary

467
00:29:53,621 --> 00:29:57,620
labor solution to replace farm workers drafted for war.

468
00:29:57,621 --> 00:30:01,620
The solution proved not so temporary.

469
00:30:01,621 --> 00:30:05,620
And eventually, nearly 5 million laborers came to the U.S.

470
00:30:05,621 --> 00:30:09,620
under the program, which ended in 1964.

471
00:30:09,621 --> 00:30:13,620
But the stream of Mexican labor continues today,

472
00:30:13,621 --> 00:30:17,620
often illegally. This ready supply of low-wage farm

473
00:30:17,621 --> 00:30:20,620
low wage farm labor often reduces the growers incentive to

474
00:30:20,621 --> 00:30:24,620
mechanize the harvest. And for the workers, the

475
00:30:24,621 --> 00:30:27,620
physical price of bending and lifting heavy loads is often

476
00:30:27,621 --> 00:30:31,620
very high. For them, a field of study known

477
00:30:31,621 --> 00:30:35,620
as ergonomics is making life better.

478
00:30:35,621 --> 00:30:38,620
Ergonomics focuses on the capabilities and limitations of

479
00:30:38,621 --> 00:30:42,620
the body in relation to a specific job, then tries to

480
00:30:42,621 --> 00:30:46,620
reduce heavy loads and prevent injuries from poor poverty

481
00:30:46,621 --> 00:30:50,860
posture and repetitive motions. We say, well, are there ways

482
00:30:50,861 --> 00:30:53,820
that we can give them better tools? Are there ways that we

483
00:30:53,821 --> 00:30:57,260
can allow them to do less bending and twisting? Are there

484
00:30:57,261 --> 00:31:03,100
ways that we can have them not lift loads at all or not lift

485
00:31:03,101 --> 00:31:07,100
so much of the loads? The hand harvesting of lettuce was

486
00:31:07,101 --> 00:31:10,140
a difficult job that ergonomic studies began targeting in the

487
00:31:10,141 --> 00:31:15,180
1970s. The mass harvest of lettuce still

488
00:31:15,181 --> 00:31:19,020
still requires a bend-and-cut motion, with the worker trimming

489
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:22,020
the outer leaves. But in the days prior to

490
00:31:22,120 --> 00:31:25,920
ergonomic study, workers packed the heads in heavy boxes and

491
00:31:26,020 --> 00:31:29,920
hand-lifted them onto trucks. Back injury was a constant

492
00:31:30,020 --> 00:31:34,040
problem. At Dole Fresh Vegetables in

493
00:31:34,140 --> 00:31:38,040
California's Salinas Valley, a harmonious union of man and

494
00:31:38,140 --> 00:31:41,980
machine increases productivity and removes the worst physical

495
00:31:41,981 --> 00:31:47,280
the worst physical stress. Like a giant factory on wheels,

496
00:31:47,281 --> 00:31:50,620
the lettuce harvester creeps slowly through the field as two

497
00:31:50,621 --> 00:31:54,700
person teams pick and package individual heads, wrapping them

498
00:31:54,701 --> 00:32:00,200
in bags and placing them in boxes. Once a box is filled, a

499
00:32:00,201 --> 00:32:03,100
power assist roller helps the worker place it on a staging

500
00:32:03,101 --> 00:32:06,340
line. The loader used to have to pick

501
00:32:06,341 --> 00:32:11,680
up the 40 to 50 pound box from foot level and put it up on top

502
00:32:11,681 --> 00:32:14,520
Now that's being done automatically by a conveyor.

503
00:32:14,620 --> 00:32:17,580
We used to have to have somebody get out there

504
00:32:17,680 --> 00:32:19,820
and staple boxes shut.

505
00:32:19,920 --> 00:32:23,120
We now have that automatic taper, which, as we've conveyed

506
00:32:23,220 --> 00:32:26,160
the box up on top of the deck, automatically shuts it

507
00:32:26,260 --> 00:32:27,660
and tapes the box shut.

508
00:32:33,700 --> 00:32:36,200
For restaurants or institutional customers

509
00:32:36,300 --> 00:32:39,200
that require unwrapped or naked lettuce,

510
00:32:39,201 --> 00:32:42,200
a second harvester uses a precision pack method.

511
00:32:45,700 --> 00:32:48,700
Here, the lettuce is actually machine packed prior to its

512
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:51,200
journey for post-harvest cooling.

513
00:33:01,700 --> 00:33:04,700
The labor-assisting devices in the lettuce harvest helped

514
00:33:04,701 --> 00:33:08,040
workers achieve an output of 5 million pounds of iceberg

515
00:33:08,041 --> 00:33:16,040
lettuce in a single week. But in some harvesting

516
00:33:16,041 --> 00:33:22,360
operations, ergonomic solutions are often surprisingly simple.

517
00:33:22,361 --> 00:33:25,360
The hand harvesting of wine grapes has changed very little

518
00:33:25,361 --> 00:33:29,700
over thousands of years. Workers cut the stems with

519
00:33:29,701 --> 00:33:34,700
hooked knives and place the bunches into bins.

520
00:33:34,701 --> 00:33:39,200
In field surveys, ergonomics researchers found that almost

521
00:33:39,300 --> 00:33:43,940
60% of grape pickers suffered from persistent back pain.

522
00:33:44,040 --> 00:33:47,140
So we said, well, what is it that we can potentially do

523
00:33:47,240 --> 00:33:49,640
about this lifting job?

524
00:33:49,740 --> 00:33:53,960
And one step is we could eliminate the lifting.

525
00:33:54,060 --> 00:33:57,760
Another step is, well, suppose we don't eliminate the lifting,

526
00:33:57,860 --> 00:34:00,800
but we just reduce the amount of weight they're lifting.

527
00:34:00,900 --> 00:34:03,400
A full bin of grapes weighs 57 pounds

528
00:34:03,401 --> 00:34:07,740
pounds on average. By designing a smaller tub to

529
00:34:07,741 --> 00:34:14,240
decrease the weight by just 11 pounds, injuries were reduced by 80%, with no noticeable change

530
00:34:14,241 --> 00:34:18,560
in productivity. The development of a mechanized

531
00:34:18,561 --> 00:34:23,720
grape mover, which mechanically lifts and empties the bins, is removing even more of the weight

532
00:34:23,721 --> 00:34:30,720
burden and speeding up the harvest. Because grapes are such a delicate fruit,

533
00:34:30,721 --> 00:34:35,720
is the desired method. However, a 400% increase in wine

534
00:34:35,721 --> 00:34:39,720
production in the last 30 years forced growers to consider a

535
00:34:39,721 --> 00:34:43,720
mechanized solution. The challenge, of course, in

536
00:34:43,721 --> 00:34:47,720
mechanically picking grapes, is in doing so without bruising or

537
00:34:47,721 --> 00:34:53,720
damaging the fruit. The Korvan Orboroder harvester

538
00:34:53,721 --> 00:34:59,720
applies gentle vibration as it passes over the vine row.

539
00:34:59,721 --> 00:35:06,720
Nylon rods with rubber cushions penetrate the dense canopy.

540
00:35:06,721 --> 00:35:10,720
The rods oscillate at 700 to 1,000 strokes per minute

541
00:35:10,721 --> 00:35:13,720
in a circular motion. The gentle vibration causes the

542
00:35:13,721 --> 00:35:17,720
right bunches to release and fall straight down without

543
00:35:17,721 --> 00:35:21,720
damaging the grapes. The idea of this is to simulate

544
00:35:21,721 --> 00:35:25,720
hand harvest to be as close as hand harvest for the premium

545
00:35:25,721 --> 00:35:28,720
and wine grapes. In this case, it's a lot of

546
00:35:28,721 --> 00:35:32,120
grapes and do the least amount of damage to the vines.

547
00:35:32,220 --> 00:35:35,120
When the grapes detach from the vines,

548
00:35:35,220 --> 00:35:38,260
a conveyor captures the bunches and transports them on a series

549
00:35:38,360 --> 00:35:40,460
of conveyors.

550
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:43,860
A fan system blasts the grapes with air to remove the leaves

551
00:35:43,960 --> 00:35:46,980
and other debris. By doing this,

552
00:35:47,080 --> 00:35:49,780
the only thing that we're picking is the berries.

553
00:35:49,880 --> 00:35:52,420
No leaves and no damage, and we're putting them straight

554
00:35:52,520 --> 00:35:55,720
into the bin. It makes this easier for the

555
00:35:55,721 --> 00:35:59,520
the winemakers because they have less foreign material to pull

556
00:35:59,521 --> 00:36:02,220
out, and it's just straight product that can go right into

557
00:36:02,221 --> 00:36:05,720
the press. Depending on the grade of wine

558
00:36:05,721 --> 00:36:08,360
and the speed setting, the harvester is capable of picking

559
00:36:08,361 --> 00:36:14,780
anywhere between 3 to 30 tons per acre in a single hour.

560
00:36:14,781 --> 00:36:17,880
Such high capacity is a big advantage, but the rougher

561
00:36:17,881 --> 00:36:21,320
handling compared to the hand harvest can cause greater damage

562
00:36:21,321 --> 00:36:24,920
to the fruit. When the juices leak from damaged

563
00:36:24,921 --> 00:36:28,220
there is a greater chance for contamination, ultimately

564
00:36:28,221 --> 00:36:31,420
affecting the winemaking process.

565
00:36:31,421 --> 00:36:34,520
What the mechanical harvester has done for grapes is even more

566
00:36:34,521 --> 00:36:38,160
pronounced in the labor-intensive raisin harvest.

567
00:36:38,161 --> 00:36:41,360
The tried-and-true method used to involve hand-picking the fruit

568
00:36:41,361 --> 00:36:44,880
and wrapping them in paper rolls for protection.

569
00:36:44,881 --> 00:36:47,980
The raisins were sun-dried in the field and periodically

570
00:36:47,981 --> 00:36:52,980
turned over several weeks before retrieval could begin.

571
00:36:54,920 --> 00:36:59,920
A new mechanized technique called continuous tray harvesting

572
00:37:00,020 --> 00:37:02,920
duplicates the technology of the wine grape harvester

573
00:37:03,020 --> 00:37:06,400
while eliminating the long and expensive process of hand-picking

574
00:37:06,500 --> 00:37:07,900
and then turning the raisins.

575
00:37:13,980 --> 00:37:16,980
The raisins are laid out on a thin sheet of paper,

576
00:37:17,080 --> 00:37:20,480
which is a quarter mile long usually and a berry and a half

577
00:37:20,580 --> 00:37:21,480
thick.

578
00:37:21,580 --> 00:37:24,180
And what this allows us to do is get a consistent

579
00:37:24,181 --> 00:37:28,320
consistent drying with no dirt and no human touching our

580
00:37:28,420 --> 00:37:30,620
raisins, and we're going for pure quality.

581
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:40,160
Cost-effective harvesting has built a strong case for

582
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:44,400
mechanization, but the struggle between low-wage labor and the

583
00:37:44,500 --> 00:37:48,240
changing face of agriculture is a battle that is far from over.

584
00:37:51,240 --> 00:37:54,180
As science pushes the envelope in the fields of robotics

585
00:37:54,181 --> 00:37:59,180
and censors, one wonders if the human hand may become obsolete

586
00:37:59,181 --> 00:38:03,080
in the coming high-tech harvest.

587
00:38:03,081 --> 00:38:06,080
Because reasons were so popular with Allied troops in World War

588
00:38:06,081 --> 00:38:11,880
II, their market price jumped from $48 per ton in 1939

589
00:38:11,881 --> 00:38:15,980
to $312 in 1946.

590
00:38:15,981 --> 00:38:17,880
Harvesting will return on Modern Marvels.

591
00:38:24,180 --> 00:38:28,080
In the boundless orange groves of Florida, hand laborers work

592
00:38:28,081 --> 00:38:30,420
long months to harvest the crop.

593
00:38:30,421 --> 00:38:33,120
The vast majority of the fruit is destined to become orange

594
00:38:33,121 --> 00:38:35,120
juice.

595
00:38:35,121 --> 00:38:38,720
Unlike many crops bound for the processing plants, these oranges

596
00:38:38,721 --> 00:38:41,240
are still mostly hand-picked.

597
00:38:41,241 --> 00:38:44,640
And that's a cause for concern among growers.

598
00:38:44,641 --> 00:38:48,140
In Brazil, cheap labor is threatening to price Florida

599
00:38:48,141 --> 00:38:50,140
right out of the business.

600
00:38:50,141 --> 00:39:02,180
Brazilians can produce orange juice and deliver it to the United States with a 20% tariff imposed on them by the United States and be cost competitive with Florida

601
00:39:02,181 --> 00:39:03,140
orange juice.

602
00:39:03,141 --> 00:39:12,680
And so the growers are looking at the real possibility that if they can't reduce their costs, they're going to be outcompeted in the market.

603
00:39:12,681 --> 00:39:17,680
They've basically decided in Florida that they cannot compete

604
00:39:17,780 --> 00:39:23,020
on a worldwide basis unless they have a mechanized harvest.

605
00:39:23,120 --> 00:39:26,020
Mechanizing the harvest would theoretically reduce

606
00:39:26,120 --> 00:39:28,420
the growers' harvesting costs.

607
00:39:28,520 --> 00:39:32,040
Until recently, an ample supply of low-wage labor kept Florida

608
00:39:32,140 --> 00:39:36,140
competitive. But the price of labor has increased,

609
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:38,380
and a crackdown on illegal immigration

610
00:39:38,480 --> 00:39:42,680
is causing a labor shortage.

611
00:39:42,681 --> 00:39:44,080
The Florida Department of Citrus

612
00:39:44,081 --> 00:39:46,520
is conducting research into the daunting task

613
00:39:46,521 --> 00:39:49,080
of mechanizing the orange harvest.

614
00:39:49,081 --> 00:39:54,080
Various canopy shakers, shake and catch,

615
00:39:54,081 --> 00:39:56,440
and trunk shaking machine prototypes

616
00:39:56,441 --> 00:39:58,680
were evaluated in field tests.

617
00:39:58,681 --> 00:40:02,080
The best are able to reduce the cost per field box

618
00:40:02,081 --> 00:40:05,920
by 20% to 50% over hand picking.

619
00:40:05,921 --> 00:40:08,180
But many obstacles remain.

620
00:40:08,181 --> 00:40:10,640
In some cases, orchards must be redesigned

621
00:40:10,641 --> 00:40:16,140
distance between trees to accommodate the large machines.

622
00:40:16,141 --> 00:40:19,640
And because so many orchards are small and independently owned,

623
00:40:19,641 --> 00:40:23,640
coordinating the mass effort will require time and patience.

624
00:40:23,641 --> 00:40:26,640
Florida oranges are but one example of the changing face

625
00:40:26,641 --> 00:40:31,640
of harvesting. Today, science is testing a host of new

626
00:40:31,641 --> 00:40:36,640
technologies to gather crops and ensure higher quality.

627
00:40:36,641 --> 00:40:40,640
Sensing technology is promising more precise quality control of

628
00:40:40,641 --> 00:40:44,640
the harvest. One scenario envisions offering

629
00:40:44,641 --> 00:40:47,640
consumers a better quality product with high-tech sugar

630
00:40:47,641 --> 00:40:51,640
sensors. So that we want to develop a

631
00:40:51,641 --> 00:40:56,640
premium label, a batch of fruit that is guaranteed to be very

632
00:40:56,641 --> 00:41:00,640
high quality, and so the harvester would collect that and

633
00:41:00,641 --> 00:41:03,640
put it in a separate container. So you would have a standard

634
00:41:03,641 --> 00:41:06,540
quality and a very high quality crop.

635
00:41:06,640 --> 00:41:08,840
Creating a cast system for fruit, of course,

636
00:41:08,940 --> 00:41:12,540
might be a mixed blessing for consumers, driving prices way up

637
00:41:12,640 --> 00:41:17,080
on the high end. But other sensor technology

638
00:41:17,180 --> 00:41:21,300
currently in development promises to bring prices down.

639
00:41:21,400 --> 00:41:25,500
A device known as the electronic nose contains 32 electric

640
00:41:25,600 --> 00:41:29,300
chemical sensors that simulate the human nose.

641
00:41:29,400 --> 00:41:33,100
By detecting the gas emissions of an orange, the nose is able to

642
00:41:33,101 --> 00:41:38,340
damage, a devastating event that ruins the value of a crop.

643
00:41:38,341 --> 00:41:40,900
Being able to separate freeze-damaged oranges from

644
00:41:40,901 --> 00:41:44,340
healthy ones could save the industry millions of dollars

645
00:41:44,341 --> 00:41:48,860
in crisis years. Cost containment has always been

646
00:41:48,861 --> 00:41:51,340
the driving force of mechanization.

647
00:41:51,341 --> 00:41:54,340
Today, it is even threatening to take the farmer out of the

648
00:41:54,341 --> 00:41:58,260
combine. Named after the Greek goddess

649
00:41:58,261 --> 00:42:02,860
of the harvest, Demeter is a computer scientist who is

650
00:42:02,861 --> 00:42:05,660
a computer-controlled harvester equipped with a pair of video

651
00:42:05,661 --> 00:42:09,460
cameras and a global positioning sensor.

652
00:42:09,461 --> 00:42:12,360
This autonomous harvester is capable of harvesting crops more

653
00:42:12,361 --> 00:42:15,360
efficiently without the fatigue issues of a human-operated

654
00:42:15,361 --> 00:42:18,960
machine. Its precise guidance system turns

655
00:42:18,961 --> 00:42:23,160
the machine to cut successive rows in a non-stop operation

656
00:42:23,161 --> 00:42:26,160
while maintaining a straight-line accuracy of plus or

657
00:42:26,161 --> 00:42:31,060
minus 3 centimeters. The video cameras ensure that

658
00:42:31,061 --> 00:42:35,560
obstacles are avoided. In the even more challenging realm

659
00:42:35,561 --> 00:42:38,860
of fruit crop harvesting, the desire to replace the human hand

660
00:42:38,861 --> 00:42:43,600
is also fueling a move into robotics.

661
00:42:43,601 --> 00:42:46,600
In labor-challenged countries from Europe to Israel,

662
00:42:46,601 --> 00:42:50,020
robotic harvesters are an enchanting vision.

663
00:42:50,021 --> 00:42:52,620
But because of high costs and slow development,

664
00:42:52,621 --> 00:42:56,560
few of the robots are in full-scale production.

665
00:42:56,561 --> 00:42:58,720
But the technology is here to stay.

666
00:42:58,721 --> 00:43:01,760
You're going to see in the future robotic techniques

667
00:43:01,761 --> 00:43:06,080
coming in that will try to simulate the quality that a

668
00:43:06,081 --> 00:43:10,080
person can harvest with. So you'll see in the future

669
00:43:10,081 --> 00:43:14,080
robots that can harvest a strawberry without bruising it,

670
00:43:14,081 --> 00:43:18,960
or harvesting a ripe peach without bruising it.

671
00:43:18,961 --> 00:43:22,960
As we peer farther into the 21st century, the tide of

672
00:43:22,961 --> 00:43:26,080
globalization will continue to play out in the farm fields,

673
00:43:26,081 --> 00:43:29,420
while machines maneuver to cut costs and satisfy production

674
00:43:29,520 --> 00:43:34,020
goals, armies of farm workers struggle to find their place in a

675
00:43:34,120 --> 00:43:38,020
changing landscape. The collision of high technology

676
00:43:38,120 --> 00:43:42,100
with tradition has always been difficult, but the revolution

677
00:43:42,200 --> 00:43:45,740
started by McCormick's famous reaper has never stopped gaining

678
00:43:45,840 --> 00:43:49,900
momentum. You have to remember that Cyrus

679
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:53,480
McCormick's epitaph was, he made bread cheap.

680
00:43:53,481 --> 00:43:56,120
And in an age when people were starving,

681
00:43:56,220 --> 00:43:59,720
someone who made bread cheap was someone who made

682
00:43:59,820 --> 00:44:01,720
an enormous contribution.

683
00:44:01,820 --> 00:44:03,720
But could Cyrus McCormick have foreseen

684
00:44:03,820 --> 00:44:08,420
the firestorm of technology his simple reaper would initiate?

685
00:44:08,520 --> 00:44:11,340
In its never-ending quest to cut costs,

686
00:44:11,440 --> 00:44:14,440
mechanization has pushed the boundaries of science

687
00:44:14,540 --> 00:44:17,580
and transformed the age-old ritual of the harvest

688
00:44:17,680 --> 00:44:21,540
into a frenzy of accumulation, a literal shakedown

689
00:44:21,541 --> 00:44:24,580
of the world's orchards and farm fields.

690
00:44:24,680 --> 00:44:26,580
What the future will bring to the harvest

691
00:44:26,680 --> 00:44:30,080
is anybody's guess.

692
00:44:30,180 --> 00:44:33,580
But the ever-evolving relationship between man, crop,

693
00:44:33,680 --> 00:44:38,300
and machine will ensure more than ever that humanity reaps

694
00:44:38,400 --> 00:44:39,700
